    "summary": "High‑energy set highlighted by blistering Dancin' In The Street and soaring Viola Lee Blues",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a blistering Dancin' In The Street and a soaring Viola Lee Blues, backed by Phil's powerful bass and forward organ work. Transitions are smooth except the sloppy China > Rider segue, and the second half maintains strong momentum.",
    "review": "On April 12 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead were in top form, kicking off with a tight run of blues‑rock before launching into a ferocious Dancin' In The Street. Jerry unleashed a snaky, blazing solo that set the tone for an evening of high‑energy improvisation, while Phil’s bass pounded out the jams and kept the groove rock solid. Pigpen’s organ stepped forward on Viola Lee Blues, Deep Elem Blues and Black Peter, adding a rare, bright texture that listeners noted as unusually prominent for this era. The band moved smoothly through Candyman into Viola Lee, but the China > Rider segue stumbled a bit, the only blemish in an otherwise fluid set. The second half kept the fire alive with a powerful Black Peter and a soulful rendition of It’s A Man’s World, closing out with a wild Feedback burst before the gentle encore “And We Bid You Good Night.” This performance captures the band at a creative peak, offering both fiery rockers and deep blues moments that make it a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
